WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.109 --> 00:00:07.508
As we walked toward St. Luke's College in the twilight, Holmes questioned Soames about who might have known the proofs were in his room.

2
00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:11.166
"Did you have any visitors after the proofs arrived?" Holmes asked.

3
00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:16.656
"Only one. Daulat Ras is an Indian student who's taking the exam," Soames said.

4
00:00:16.657 --> 00:00:20.379
"He lives two floors above me and had a quick question about the exam."

5
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.238
"Hmm." Holmes thought for a moment.

6
00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:27.028
"Is it possible he recognized the papers on your desk as proofs?"

7
00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:31.693
"Probably not. They were still rolled up because I hadn't started reading them yet."

8
00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:34.494
"Hmm," Holmes said again.

9
00:00:34.495 --> 00:00:39.173
"What about Bannister? Could he have known about the proofs before he entered your rooms?"

10
00:00:39.174 --> 00:00:40.989
Soames thought for a moment.

11
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:44.194
"He's worked at the college for many years.

12
00:00:44.195 --> 00:00:48.727
"So, he's probably aware that they arrive the day before the exam starts.

13
00:00:48.728 --> 00:00:53.114
But he's quite busy with other things. I doubt he knew they were there."

14
00:00:53.115 --> 00:00:57.568
"I think we'll find that whoever copied the proofs came upon them accidentally, and—"

15
00:00:57.569 --> 00:01:01.267
Holmes paused dramatically. "It was a student."

16
00:01:01.268 --> 00:01:05.960
"Oh, dear." Soames removed his hat and ran a nervous hand through his hair.

17
00:01:05.961 --> 00:01:09.746
We walked through the main gates of St. Luke's and entered a large courtyard.

18
00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:13.084
Soames pointed out his rooms, which were on the ground floor.

19
00:01:13.085 --> 00:01:17.465
Next to his windows, an arched doorway led to a worn stone staircase.

20
00:01:17.466 --> 00:01:20.499
"How many students live above you?" Holmes asked.

21
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.748
"Just three." Soames proceeded to tell us about them.

22
00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:27.049
Richard Gilchrist lived on the floor above Soames.

23
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.810
A scholar and athlete, he was on the college rugby and cricket teams.

24
00:01:31.811 --> 00:01:35.991
He also competed in hurdles and long jump for the track and field team.

25
00:01:35.992 --> 00:01:38.651
"You may have heard of his father," Soames said. "The

26
00:01:38.652 --> 00:01:43.769
senior Gilchrist was in the news because he spent all the family money before he died." "I

27
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.606
do recall something about that," I said. "Young

28
00:01:46.607 --> 00:01:49.992
Gilchrist was left almost penniless, as I remember." "That's

29
00:01:49.993 --> 00:01:54.916
correct, but I think Gilchrist will do well in life because he's a hard worker." Soames

30
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:58.062
continued describing the students who lived above him. Daulat

31
00:01:58.063 --> 00:02:01.650
Ras' room was above Gilchrist's, on the next floor. He

32
00:02:01.651 --> 00:02:04.351
was a quiet student, steady and methodical. Although

33
00:02:04.352 --> 00:02:09.105
he got good grades, Ras believed Greek was his weakest subject. Miles

34
00:02:10.305 --> 00:02:12.797
McLaren lived on the top floor. He

35
00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:17.132
was a brilliant student when he chose to work, but he'd been slacking off all term. "He's

36
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:20.647
probably dreading the exam," Soames said. "And

37
00:02:20.648 --> 00:02:27.599
here's something else you might want to know: he was nearly expelled for cheating at cards during his first year at the college." "So

38
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.254
you think McLaren is the most likely suspect?" Holmes said. Soames'

39
00:02:31.255 --> 00:02:36.106
hands fluttered in protest. "I wouldn't want to accuse anyone. But

40
00:02:36.107 --> 00:02:39.244
McLaren is probably the one most likely to cheat." As

41
00:02:39.245 --> 00:02:43.700
we approached Soames' rooms, Holmes stood on his tiptoes and looked through the window. "You'll

42
00:02:43.701 --> 00:02:47.882
notice that the window has many small panes," Soames said. "There's

43
00:02:47.883 --> 00:02:53.018
no way anyone could fit through those spaces, so the culprit must have used the door." Holmes

44
00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:01.340
raised his eyebrows at me, and I had to hide a smile at Soames' observation, which was so obvious to the great detective and me. As

45
00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:06.757
there was nothing more to be learned outside, we went into the building, and Soames unlocked his door. While

46
00:03:06.758 --> 00:03:10.697
Holmes examined the sitting room, the two of us waited in the doorway. "There

47
00:03:10.698 --> 00:03:13.759
are no footprints on the carpet," Holmes said. "But

48
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.875
I didn't think I'd find any on such a dry day. Now,

49
00:03:16.876 --> 00:03:19.597
where did you leave your servant?" "Bannister

50
00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:21.890
was sitting in my desk chair." "Interesting.

51
00:03:21.891 --> 00:03:27.468
Well, you can come in now that I've finished with the carpet." Holmes motioned to us. "I

52
00:03:27.469 --> 00:03:34.248
think I know what happened. The student took the pages one by one over to the small table by the window. "From

53
00:03:34.818 --> 00:03:38.003
there, he could watch the courtyard for your return. Then

54
00:03:38.004 --> 00:03:41.213
he could make his escape before you found him in your rooms." "I

55
00:03:41.214 --> 00:03:47.663
hate to contradict you," Soames said, "but he couldn't see me in the courtyard because I entered through a side gate." "The

56
00:03:47.664 --> 00:03:50.711
student had no way of knowing that," Holmes snapped. "I

57
00:03:50.712 --> 00:03:54.287
think he took the first page over to the table and began copying it. Now,

58
00:03:54.288 --> 00:03:57.904
how long would that take? Maybe 15 minutes? Then

59
00:03:57.905 --> 00:04:00.909
he tossed it on the floor and picked up the next page. He

60
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.747
was in the middle of that when your sudden return caused him to rush away." "And

61
00:04:04.748 --> 00:04:09.120
he didn't have time to put the two pages back on the desk," I said. "Very

62
00:04:09.972 --> 00:04:14.141
good, Watson." Holmes beamed as if I were his prize pupil. "But

63
00:04:14.142 --> 00:04:18.956
I didn't hear anyone running up the stairs as I entered," Soames protested. Holmes

64
00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:25.166
ignored him. "At any rate, he was writing so furiously that he broke his pencil and had to sharpen it. This

65
00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:31.278
pencil was unusual—dark blue with the manufacturer's name in silver lettering—and very short. Only

66
00:04:31.279 --> 00:04:33.452
a couple inches remained." Holmes

67
00:04:33.453 --> 00:04:37.244
held out a shaving. "Do you see what I mean, Soames?" "I'm

68
00:04:37.245 --> 00:04:40.159
afraid I don't," Soames said. Holmes

69
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.388
gave an exasperated sigh and pointed to the letters NN with a space after them. "You are

70
00:04:45.903 --> 00:04:50.296
aware that Johann Faber is the most common manufacturer of pencils? This pencil

71
00:04:50.297 --> 00:04:55.048
was made by Faber—the N s come from the first name Johann ."

72
00:04:55.527 --> 00:04:58.957
Holmes raised his eyebrows at me again, but I said nothing.

73
00:04:58.958 --> 00:05:05.635
He examined the wood shavings on the little table and concluded that the student had used a large blunt knife to sharpen the pencil.

74
00:05:05.636 --> 00:05:09.820
Holmes moved over to the desk, where he picked up and examined a small black lump.

75
00:05:09.821 --> 00:05:12.760
"I assume this is the strange clay that you spoke of."

76
00:05:12.761 --> 00:05:17.483
Soames nodded, but Holmes was already studying the cut on the leather desktop.

77
00:05:17.484 --> 00:05:21.079
It began with a tiny scratch and ended in a jagged hole.

78
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.707
"A most unusual cut." Holmes pointed to a door.

79
00:05:24.708 --> 00:05:26.461
"Now, where does this lead to?"

80
00:05:26.462 --> 00:05:28.814
"My bedroom," Soames said.

81
00:05:28.815 --> 00:05:32.750
"And have you been in there since you discovered someone had tampered with the proofs?"

82
00:05:32.751 --> 00:05:35.440
"No, I came straight to you."

83
00:05:36.126 --> 00:05:38.416
"Then I would like to have a look around."

84
00:05:38.417 --> 00:05:41.371
Holmes held out his arm to bar us from the room.

85
00:05:41.372 --> 00:05:44.195
"Please allow me to examine the floor before you enter."

86
00:05:44.196 --> 00:05:47.916
He looked all around the room but once again found no footprints.

87
00:05:47.917 --> 00:05:50.552
"I assume you hang your clothes behind this curtain."

88
00:05:50.553 --> 00:05:53.616
Holmes gestured toward a piece of fabric along one wall.

89
00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:57.870
"If anyone was forced to hide himself in this room, he'd do it there.

90
00:05:57.871 --> 00:06:01.056
The bed is too low, and the wardrobe is too shallow.

91
00:06:01.057 --> 00:06:03.690
Shall we see if anyone is hiding behind the curtain?"

92
00:06:03.691 --> 00:06:07.851
My heart began to pound as I noticed the wary look on Holmes' face.

93
00:06:07.852 --> 00:06:13.946
I knew that my friend fully expected to pull back the curtain and find the culprit still hiding there!

